WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Heap use in constrained C should be governed by a documented policy.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.960
Some systems avoid dynamic allocation after initialization.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.220
Others allow it only in specific subsystems.

4
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.680
Some use memory pools instead of a general heap.

5
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:22.180
The important point is that reviewers can tell what is allowed and why.

6
00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:25.100
Allocation failure is normal design input.

7
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:33.360
The code should check allocation results, preserve safe state, release resources it already owns, and return a clear error to the caller.

8
00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:39.780
A function that cannot obtain memory should not partially update shared state and then continue as though the operation succeeded.

9
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.020
Fragmentation matters in long-running systems.

10
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.360
Even if total free memory looks adequate, the allocator may not have a large enough contiguous block for a later request.

11
00:00:49.460 --> 00:01:00.500
Designs with repeated allocate and free cycles should be reviewed for object sizes, lifetimes, and whether a pool or fixed ownership pattern would be more predictable.

12
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:05.020
Memory pools can clarify limits and ownership when they are designed carefully.

13
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:11.840
A pool can cap the number of active objects, avoid some fragmentation patterns, and make exhaustion behavior visible.

14
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.220
It still needs rules for borrowing, returning, resetting, and handling pool exhaustion.

15
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.860
Cleanup paths deserve the same quality as normal paths.

16
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:28.400
If a function allocates several objects and fails in the middle, it must release the objects it owns and leave the caller with a consistent result.

17
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:34.420
Reviewers should trace allocation ownership across every return path, not only the success path.

18
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.820
Avoid allocating during critical operations when possible.

19
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:49.520
If an operation controls safety, persistence, startup, update commit, or time-sensitive work, it may be better to preallocate required resources or prove that allocation failure is handled safely.

20
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.620
The design should not depend on memory appearing at the most stressful moment.

21
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:58.300
Testing should force allocation failure at different points.

22
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.520
A normal-path test that never exhausts memory tells only part of the story.

23
00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:11.620
Targeted failure tests reveal whether cleanup, retries, backpressure, and diagnostics behave predictably when memory is unavailable.
